  "body": "## Start where the code is\nClaude Code works on the folder you start it in. That folder is the session's working directory: Claude reads every file under it without asking, and loads the CLAUDE.md files in it and above it. So open the terminal in the repo root first.\n\n```powershell\ncd C:\\code\\orders-web\nclaude\n```\n\nYou can hand it the first task straight away: `claude \"read ROADMAP.md and tell me which phase is next\"`.\n\n## Five ways to start\n| You type | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| `claude` | A new conversation in this folder |\n| `claude \"task\"` | A new conversation that starts on the task |\n| `claude -c` | Continue the most recent conversation in this folder |\n| `claude -r \"search-phase-2\"` | Resume a named conversation (with no name: a picker) |\n| `claude -n \"search-phase-2\"` | A new conversation with a name you can resume later |\n\n`claude -p \"question\"` is different: it answers once, prints and exits, with no chance to steer it. It is for scripts and pipes, such as `git diff main | claude -p \"summarise this diff\"`.\n\n## The prompt box\nThree characters change what the box does when they come first, and `@` works anywhere:\n\n| Type | It means |\n|---|---|\n| `/` at the start | A command or a skill: `/clear`, `/model`, `/crisp` |\n| `!` at the start | Shell mode: you run the command and its output goes into the conversation |\n| `?` on an empty box | Show or hide the shortcut help |\n| `@` anywhere | Mention a file or folder, with autocomplete |\n\nFor a prompt over several lines, press **Shift+Enter** in Windows Terminal, **Ctrl+J** in any terminal, or end the line with `\\` and press Enter.\n\n## While Claude works\n- **Esc** stops it. What it did so far is kept, so you can redirect it.\n- Type and press **Enter** to queue a message without interrupting. Claude reads it as soon as its current tool calls finish, in the same turn.\n- **Ctrl+C** with nothing running clears the box; press it again to exit.\n\n## When something is off\n`claude --version` shows the version (this tool was checked against 2.1.309), `claude doctor` checks the installation and your settings, and `claude update` installs the latest. Inside a session, `/doctor` runs a check-up that can propose fixes, and `/status` shows which settings files loaded.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Starting Claude in your home folder instead of the repo, so it cannot see the project or its CLAUDE.md.\n- Starting a fresh session every morning and re-explaining yesterday: `claude -c` or `claude -r <name>` brings it back.\n- Using `claude -p` for real work: it runs one prompt and gives you no way to steer it.",
