  "body": "## Why it matters\nClaude does its best work from evidence. \"The tests are failing\" makes it guess which tests and why; the real output does not. Two prefixes put evidence into the conversation without a round of \"can you run…\".\n\n## ! runs a command for you\nStart the prompt with `!` and what follows runs as a shell command, straight away, without Claude interpreting or approving it:\n\n```text\n!cargo test -p orders -q\n!pnpm vitest run src/cart --reporter=dot\n!git status\n```\n\n- The command and its output go into the conversation, so Claude sees exactly what you saw.\n- Claude then responds to the output on its own; that reply costs the same as a normal prompt. To add output without a reply, set `respondToBashCommands` to `false` in settings.\n- **Ctrl+B** sends a long run to the background.\n- **Tab** completes from the `!` commands you ran before in this project.\n- Esc, Backspace or Ctrl+U on an empty prompt leaves shell mode.\n\nKeep the output short. Every line stays in the context, so a 3,000-line log costs as much as if Claude had read the file. Use quiet flags such as `cargo test -q` or `--reporter=dot`.\n\n## @ points at the file\nType `@` and a path anywhere in the prompt; autocomplete helps:\n\n```text\nExplain the retry logic in @src/payments/client.rs and where it is tested\nWhy does @src/search/api.ts return 500 when the query is empty?\n```\n\n- A file mention puts the file's content into the conversation when it fits the Read tool's limit: 25,000 tokens by default, and text files over 256 KB are left out.\n- A folder mention gives the list of files, not their content: `What is in @src/components?`\n- Mentioning a file also loads the CLAUDE.md files in its folder and the folders above it.\n- Several mentions in one message are fine.\n- A `Read` deny rule, such as one for `.env`, keeps that file out even if you mention it.\n\n## Which one, when\n| You want Claude to know | Use |\n|---|---|\n| What a command prints right now | `!` and the command |\n| What a file says | `@` and the path |\n| Something it has to find out | Ask in words; it will search |\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Describing an error in your own words instead of running the command that shows it.\n- \"Look at the search API file\" instead of `@src/search/api.ts`: Claude may open the wrong one.\n- Running a command with thousands of lines of output in shell mode: all of it stays in the context.",
