  "body": "## What a mode is\nEvery action Claude takes, such as reading a file, editing one or running a command, goes through a permission check. The mode is the baseline answer: which actions go ahead and which wait for you. Permission rules, in the next lessons, adjust it for named commands and files.\n\n## The six modes\n| Mode | Status line | Runs without asking | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Manual (`default`) | `⏸ manual mode on` | Reads only | Unfamiliar or production code |\n| Accept edits (`acceptEdits`) | `⏵⏵ accept edits on` | Reads, file edits, and `mkdir`, `touch`, `mv`, `cp` in your working folders | Building a plan you approved |\n| Plan (`plan`) | `⏸ plan mode on` | Reads and exploring; no edits until you approve a plan | Anything bigger than a small fix |\n| Auto (`auto`) | `⏵⏵ auto mode on` | Almost everything, with a classifier checking each action | Long tasks in your own repo |\n| Don't ask (`dontAsk`) | `⏵⏵ don't ask on` | Only what your allow rules pre-approve; the rest is refused | Scripts and CI |\n| Bypass (`bypassPermissions`) | `⏵⏵ bypass permissions on` | Everything | Throwaway containers and VMs only |\n\nTwo things hold in every mode: **deny rules always block**, bypass included, and a built-in set of read-only commands (`ls`, `cat`, `grep`, `git status` and the like) never prompts you.\n\n## Switching\n- **Shift+Tab** cycles Manual, Accept edits, Plan, then bypass (only if the session started with it enabled) and auto. Watch the status line at the bottom.\n- `claude --permission-mode plan` starts in a mode; `/plan` switches for one task. Don't ask is set only with `--permission-mode dontAsk`.\n- `permissions.defaultMode` in a settings file sets where sessions start. On 2.1.283 and later, interactive sessions start in **auto** unless a setting says otherwise; `claude -p` starts in Manual.\n\nYour own setup: both of your settings files set `\"defaultMode\": \"auto\"`, so every session on your machine starts in auto mode. For a repo you are just getting to know, press Shift+Tab to Manual or Plan first.\n\n## Which mode, when\n1. A new repo, or code you do not know: **Plan** to read first, or **Manual**.\n2. A change bigger than one file: **Plan**, read the plan, then approve it.\n3. Building the plan you approved: **Accept edits** (commit first, so git can take you back) or **Auto**.\n4. A long, well-defined job in your own repo: **Auto**, with your limits said out loud (\"don't push\").\n5. A script or CI job: **Don't ask**, with allow rules for exactly what it needs.\n6. **Bypass**: only in a container with nothing of yours in it.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Not looking at the status line, and assuming Claude will ask before it edits.\n- Using bypass on your own machine \"just for this task\": it skips every prompt, including writes to `.git` and `.claude`.\n- Leaving a CI job in a mode that waits for an answer nobody will give. Use `dontAsk` with allow rules.",
