  "body": "## How it works\nIn auto mode Claude works without routine prompts. Before an action such as a shell command or a network request runs, a separate classifier model checks it against what you asked for. It blocks anything that goes beyond your request, targets infrastructure it does not recognise, or looks driven by hostile content Claude read (prompt injection). Tool results are stripped from what the classifier sees, so a malicious file cannot talk it round.\n\nOn Claude Code 2.1.283 and later, auto is where interactive sessions start unless a setting says otherwise, and your settings set it explicitly.\n\n## Blocked by default (a selection)\n- Downloading and running code, such as `curl … | bash`\n- Force push; `git reset --hard`, `git checkout -- .`, `git clean -fd`, `git stash drop`\n- Production deploys and migrations; `terraform destroy`\n- Sending sensitive data to outside endpoints; granting IAM or repo permissions\n- Destroying files that existed before the session\n- Merging a pull request no human has approved\n\n## Allowed by default (a selection)\n- File work in your working folder\n- Installing the dependencies your lock files or manifests declare\n- Read-only HTTP requests\n- Pushing to a branch of the repo you are working in\n\n## Your words count\nThe classifier judges actions against your request, so say the limits out loud: \"fix the lint errors; don't push anything\" makes a push an escalation. Explicit `ask` rules still make it ask you, and deny rules still block.\n\n## When it hands back to you\nIf the classifier blocks 3 actions in a row, or 20 in total, auto mode pauses and Claude Code goes back to asking you. Blocked actions are listed in `/permissions` under **Recently denied**, where pressing `r` retries one with your approval.\n\nEntering auto mode also drops broad allow rules that would let anything run: `Bash(*)`, wildcarded interpreters such as `Bash(python*)`, and package-manager run rules such as `Bash(npm run *)`. Narrow rules such as `Bash(npm test)` stay, and the dropped ones come back when you leave auto mode.\n\n## It is not a guarantee\nThe docs put it plainly: auto mode reduces prompts but does not guarantee safety. Use it where you trust the general direction. For production systems, secrets and anything you cannot undo, stay in Manual or Plan and read each step. A rewind undoes file edits, never a push, a migration or a deploy.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Treating auto mode as a sandbox. It is a reviewer, not a wall.\n- Giving a vague task (\"clean up the repo\") and being surprised by what counted as in scope.\n- Ignoring Recently denied after a pause: the blocked action may be one you need.",
