  "body": "## Four ways to split off\n| You want | Use | What you get |\n|---|---|---|\n| Another approach here, keeping the original | `/branch try-redis` | A copy of the conversation, and you switch into it |\n| The same, from the terminal | `claude -c --fork-session` | A new session id with the old history |\n| Another approach in parallel while you carry on | `/fork` and a prompt | A copy running as a background session |\n| To undo the last few steps instead | `/rewind` | The same conversation, rolled back |\n\n## /branch in practice\n```text\n/branch try-streaming\n```\n\n- The branch gets the conversation up to now. The original stays unchanged on disk and in the picker; `/resume` and its name take you back.\n- \"Allow for this session\" approvals carry over, because the branch runs in the same process. A `--fork-session` copy is a new process, so you approve again there.\n- Background jobs that were running keep running, and their output lands in the branch.\n\n## Branch or rewind?\nRewind when the last steps were simply wrong and you will not need them. Branch when both paths might be worth keeping, for example two designs you want to compare before choosing.\n\n## Files are shared\nA branch copies the conversation, not your files. Both branches edit the same working tree, so commit or stash before you switch between them. For two lines of work whose files must not touch, use a git worktree: `claude -w <name>` (batch 4 covers worktrees).\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Branching to undo a mistake: `/rewind` is the tool for that.\n- Two branches editing the same files, then losing track of which change came from where.\n- Resuming one session in two terminals instead of branching it.",
