  "body": "## The test for every line\nAsk: *would removing this make Claude make mistakes?* If not, cut it. A bloated CLAUDE.md costs context in every session and buries the rules that matter, so Claude follows all of them less well.\n\n## Include and leave out\n| Include | Leave out |\n|---|---|\n| Commands Claude cannot guess: build, test, one test, lint | What Claude can work out by reading the code |\n| Style rules that differ from the defaults | The language's standard conventions |\n| How tests are run and written here | Detailed API docs (link to them instead) |\n| Branch names, commit and PR conventions | Anything that changes often |\n| Architecture decisions specific to this repo | Long explanations and tutorials |\n| Environment quirks: required variables, ports | A file-by-file description of the codebase |\n| Gotchas that are not obvious | \"Write clean code\" and other self-evident advice |\n\n## Make each rule checkable\n| Vague | Concrete |\n|---|---|\n| Format code properly | `pnpm prettier --write .` owns formatting; do not hand-format |\n| Test your changes | Run `pnpm vitest run <file>` for the file you changed, and `pnpm test` before committing |\n| Keep files organised | API handlers live in `src/api/handlers/`, one file per route |\n| Handle errors well | Handlers return `AppError`; no `unwrap()` in request code |\n\n## Size and shape\n- Aim for **under 200 lines** per file. Past that, move topic rules into `.claude/rules/` ([[lesson:imports-and-rules|next lesson]]) and workflows into skills.\n- Headings and short bullets, not paragraphs.\n- **IMPORTANT** on one line works; on ten lines, nothing stands out.\n- No secrets, ever: the file is in git and in every session's context.\n- Skip style rules a formatter or linter already enforces (prettier, eslint, rustfmt, clippy). Let the tool own them; a hook can run it after every edit (batch 3).\n- HTML comments are stripped before Claude sees the file, so notes for people cost no context.\n\n## Keep it alive\nAdd a line when Claude makes the same mistake twice, when a review catches something Claude should have known, or when you type the same correction you typed last session. Remove a line when it is no longer needed. `/doctor` proposes cuts for a checked-in CLAUDE.md, and `/doctor prompt-audit` looks for outdated and conflicting instructions.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Pasting the README in. Import it, or leave it out.\n- Ten \"MUST\" lines in capitals.\n- A rule that only matters for migrations, loaded in every session about the UI.",
