  "body": "## CLAUDE.md is yours; auto memory is Claude's\n| | CLAUDE.md | Auto memory |\n|---|---|---|\n| Written by | You | Claude |\n| Holds | Instructions and rules | What it learned: your preferences, corrections, project facts |\n| Scope | Your organisation, you, a project | One git repo and its worktrees, on this machine only |\n| Loaded | Every session, in full | Every session: the first 200 lines or 25 KB of MEMORY.md |\n\n## What Claude saves\nFour kinds of note, each a small Markdown file with a `type` in its frontmatter:\n\n- **user**: your role and working preferences\n- **feedback**: corrections you gave and approaches you confirmed\n- **project**: decisions and deadlines it cannot read from the code or git\n- **reference**: where to find things outside the repo, such as a dashboard\n\nIt skips what the code or your CLAUDE.md already says, and it does not save something every session.\n\n## Where it lives\n`~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/`:\n\n```text\nMEMORY.md            the index: one line per memory, loaded every session\nfeedback_testing.md  one memory, read when needed\nuser_role.md         another\n```\n\nOnly the index loads at the start; topic files are read when needed. When Claude says \"Saved 2 memories\" or \"Recalled 2 memories\", it is using this folder. Your own setup already uses it: the notes about this tool's batches live in one.\n\n## Talking to it\n- \"Remember that we use pnpm, not npm\" saves a note to auto memory.\n- \"Add this to CLAUDE.md\" puts it in the instructions instead. Use that for rules the team needs.\n- `/memory` lists your CLAUDE.md files, switches auto memory on or off, and opens the memory folder. Everything there is plain Markdown: read it, fix it, delete what is wrong.\n\n## Turning it off\nThe `/memory` switch writes `autoMemoryEnabled` to your user settings. Set the same key in a project's settings to turn it off for one repo, or `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` for one run. CI jobs and shared machines are the usual cases.\n\n## Common mistakes\n- Saying \"remember this\" for a team rule: the note stays on your machine. Put it in CLAUDE.md.\n- Never looking at what was saved, then wondering why Claude holds on to an old preference.\n- Letting MEMORY.md grow past 200 lines: the rest does not load.",
